From fad9daa91d10d0609d418745578d9f75d4744fd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vita-build <vita@build>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:35:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Vita: the PS1 3D path by default, drawn at the screen's
 resolution; t16's overlap test removed; the game thread on core 0

The native renderer's draws cost the Vita's CPU more than the PS1 path does (t15 38 fps, t16
less), so on the Vita 3D goes the PS1 way by default again; THPS_RENDER3D=native brings the
native renderer back (elsewhere the default stays native).

The screens are no longer fixed at the PS1's resolution: the display surfaces are drawn at the
size they are shown at, 4:3 within 960x544 (728x544, a whole number of 8 pixels so a surface's
texture rows are its width and it is shown 1:1), with the vertices' sub-pixel positions;
THPS_GL_SCALE=n draws them at n times the PS1's resolution again (THPS_GL_SCALE=1: as before).
Scaled up, the geometry doesn't move by half a pixel (u_shift 0), so the ES 2 shaders take texels
at the pixel's centre instead of adding half a texel (u_shift goes to the fragment stage in
v_flags.w). Reading the screens back (ReadPixels, ReadArea) on the Vita reads texels where they
are after a glFinish, for the surfaces and the readback texture too, as for the base before:
vitaGL's glReadPixels takes a framebuffer's rows from the far end.

t16's per-triangle overlap test cost 20-40 ms of CPU a frame and split the native renderer's
draws no less: back to the face's box and depth range. The layers' vertex buffers stay.

The game thread (the main thread) keeps to core 0: with all three cores allowed it was moved
about 150 times a second.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
